/-
QOU.Mass.BindingMassRung — the mass/binding ℏ_q-rung dichotomy (formalize-first core).

Arc context (bk-crossing-coordination, item 3b→1 crux; PR #5346). The forward test
(`docs/audits/2026-08-21-binding-hbarq-rung-forward-test.md`) established two multiplier laws at
fixed hyperbolic volume:

  mass    :  m(K) = m_e · Vol(K) · (lw / ℏ_q²)     lw = linking_weight, O(1) at the classical point
  binding :  B(K) = m_e · Vol(K) · M               M  = coordination count, O(1)

The `1/ℏ_q²` in the mass multiplier is the CAL-2 muon-anchor cusp factor (`m_μ/m_e = Vol(4₁)/ℏ_q²`,
`ℏ_q = 1 − q₀⁻¹`); the binding multiplier carries no such factor. This file formalizes the analytic
DICHOTOMY that separates the two rungs at the classical point `ℏ_q → 0` (`q → 1`):

  • the mass multiplier `lw/ℏ_q²` is an order-2 pole with nonzero residue `lw`
    (rest mass is generated by the deformation being small — it diverges as ℏ_q → 0);
  • the binding/mass ratio is exactly `(M/lw)·ℏ_q²`, an O(ℏ_q²) quantity that vanishes as ℏ_q → 0
    (nuclear binding is an O(ℏ_q²) quantum correction — it is regular at the classical point).

This RIGOROUSLY states the "one rung below" observation. It does NOT derive *why* binding lacks the
cusp factor — that residual kernel is the open item (bean qou-…; see the audit). All statements are
over generic `ℝ`; `ℏ_q`, `lw`, `M` are treated as real parameters (the archimedean evaluation).
-/
import Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real
import Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.Order.Field

namespace QOU.Mass.BindingMassRung

open Filter Topology

/-- Mass multiplier at deformation `hbar := ℏ_q`: the O(1) linking weight `lw` over the cusp
factor `ℏ_q²`. -/
noncomputable def massMultiplier (lw hbar : ℝ) : ℝ := lw / hbar ^ 2

/-- Binding-to-mass ratio at deformation `hbar`: the coordination count `M` over the mass
multiplier. -/
noncomputable def bindingOverMass (M lw hbar : ℝ) : ℝ := M / massMultiplier lw hbar

/-- **Binding/mass is exactly `(M/lw)·ℏ_q²`.** The ratio is a pure order-`ℏ_q²` quantity — the
rigorous form of "binding sits one ℏ_q² rung below mass." -/
theorem bindingOverMass_eq (M lw hbar : ℝ) (hlw : lw ≠ 0) :
    bindingOverMass M lw hbar = (M / lw) * hbar ^ 2 := by
  unfold bindingOverMass massMultiplier
  field_simp

/-- **The mass multiplier is an order-2 pole with residue `lw`:** weighting by `ℏ_q²` recovers
exactly the O(1) linking weight. Since `lw ≠ 0`, the pole is genuine (rest mass diverges as
`ℏ_q → 0`). -/
theorem massMultiplier_pole_residue (lw hbar : ℝ) (hb : hbar ≠ 0) :
    hbar ^ 2 * massMultiplier lw hbar = lw := by
  unfold massMultiplier
  field_simp

/-- **Binding/mass → 0 as `ℏ_q → 0`** (classical point): nuclear binding is a quantum correction
that vanishes classically, while rest mass (the pole above) diverges there. -/
theorem bindingOverMass_tendsto_zero (M lw : ℝ) (hlw : lw ≠ 0) :
    Tendsto (fun hbar => bindingOverMass M lw hbar) (𝓝 0) (𝓝 0) := by
  have h : (fun hbar => bindingOverMass M lw hbar) = fun hbar => (M / lw) * hbar ^ 2 := by
    funext hbar; exact bindingOverMass_eq M lw hbar hlw
  rw [h]
  have : Tendsto (fun hbar : ℝ => (M / lw) * hbar ^ 2) (𝓝 0) (𝓝 ((M / lw) * 0 ^ 2)) :=
    (tendsto_const_nhds.mul ((continuous_pow 2).tendsto 0))
  simpa using this

end QOU.Mass.BindingMassRung
